For a simple compressible mixture at equilibrium, write the fundamental differential with the actual work modes and species variables. Under the usual restricted mechanical model, dU = T dS−p dV+Σμ_i dn_i. Additional electrical, surface, elastic or magnetic terms require explicit definitions.

Legendre transforms exchange natural variables: H = U+pV, F = U−TS and G = U+pV−TS. Their differentials reveal which derivatives yield temperature, pressure, entropy, volume and chemical potentials. A minimum-potential criterion applies under its matching constraints and contact with the specified reservoirs, not under every process condition.

Derive the four differentials and use one consistent state model to calculate the same measurable response in two representations. Explain why minimizing G at fixed temperature/pressure is different from conserving energy in an isolated system. The shared chemical-direction Record provides the first concrete example of this broader structure.

